+## Prior work vs. hackathon work
+
+The hackathon allows building on an existing project if only the new work is showcased.
+
+**Before the hackathon** (commits up to `0d3dde1`, 2026-10-19): the seven-agent pipeline, the
+hardened Docker sandbox, the verifier, the dashboard, the mock provider, a Meta provider written to
+the documented contract but never run, five planted defects (D1–D5) and a one-shot benchmark. No
+real model had been run.
+
+**During the hackathon** (from 2026-10-27):
+
+* First real runs on **Muse Spark 1.3**, and the fixes they exposed (truncated replies retried with
+  a higher limit; runtime failures no longer scored as evidence against correct predictions).
+* **Two Meta models in two roles**: Muse Spark 1.3 forms hypotheses; open-weights **Muse Spark
+  Open** (Baseten) designs experiments and writes repairs.
+* A **fair baseline** (edit → run tests → retry, same call budget) and a three-way benchmark with
+  hidden-test scoring and a live spend guard.
+* **Harder defects** D6–D9, each designed to defeat a one-shot fix, and a **real open-source bug**
+  (R1, `parse` issue #162) that made the **pytest** runner real.
+* **Flaky-test triage** and a verifier that adapts its re-runs to it.
+* **GitHub integration**: `forge0-ci` and a workflow template.
+* Ranked context selection so root causes two imports away are not cut.
+
+## Use of Meta AI
+
+* **Muse Spark 1.3** via the Meta Model API: JSON-schema structured outputs, `reasoning_effort`,
+  the `max_completion_tokens` contract, usage accounting including cached and reasoning tokens.
+* **Muse Spark Open** (`meta-models/muse-spark-open-1026`) on Baseten through the
+  OpenAI-compatible provider, routed per task, so the open-weights model does the high-volume work
+  (experiment plans, repairs) and the hosted model does the hypothesis reasoning.
+
+## Results (real, 2026-10-27)
+
+Muse Spark 1.3, 10 defects × 3 repeats, scored by hidden tests no system saw
+([EVALUATION.md](EVALUATION.md)):
+
+| | One-shot | Edit-test loop | FORGE-0 |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Fixed | 28/30 | 30/30 | 30/30 |
+| False fix claims | 2 | 0 | 0 |
+| Cost per investigation | $0.012 | $0.013 | $0.071 |
+
+Said plainly: an edit-test loop matches FORGE-0's fix rate on this set at a fifth of the cost. The
+one-shot baseline is the one that ships broken fixes. FORGE-0 adds the evidence behind each fix,
+which is what lets a reviewer merge a bot's pull request with confidence. The two-model
+configuration fixed both defects it was run on (D6, R1).
+
+We then pre-registered three harder defects and an open-weights arm, with predictions, before
+running them ([benchmarks/preregistration](../benchmarks/preregistration/2026-10-27-harder-defects.md)).
+On Muse Spark 1.3 all three systems fixed D10–D12 9/9, so the predictions were wrong. With Muse
+Spark Open as the only model, the loop fixed 38/39 (one false claim) and FORGE-0 30/39 (none):
+seven FORGE-0 runs died in the experiment-design call, where the model used its whole token budget
+reasoning. FORGE-0 does not beat the loop on fix rate on anything we measured; we report that
+rather than re-tune the defects.
+
